.TH HSH 1 "September 2026" "hsh 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hsh \- a basic UNIX command-line interpreter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ./hsh
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hsh
is a simple command-line interpreter written in C.
It reads commands from standard input and runs them one line at a time.
The shell can be used interactively from a terminal or non-interactively
through a pipe or redirected input.
.PP
Each input line is split into tokens at spaces and tabs.
The first token becomes the command name in
.BR argv[0] ,
and the remaining tokens become its arguments.
Built-in commands are handled directly by the shell.
For external commands, the shell resolves the command path before calling
.BR fork (2).
The child calls
.BR execve (2),
while the parent waits for the child to finish.
.SH INTERACTIVE MODE
When standard input is connected to a terminal,
.B hsh
displays the following prompt:
.PP
.RS
.B shell$
.RE
.PP
Enter one command per line and press Enter to run it.
Enter
.B exit
to close the shell.
Pressing Ctrl+D at an empty prompt causes the shell to encounter
end-of-file and exit.
.SH NON-INTERACTIVE MODE
When standard input comes from a pipe or redirected file,
.B hsh
does not display a prompt.
It reads and executes each input line until it reaches end-of-file.
.PP
A single command can be piped into the shell:
.PP
.nf
$ echo "ls -l /tmp" | ./hsh
.fi
.PP
Multiple commands must be separated by newline characters:
.PP
.nf
$ printf "pwd\\nls -l\\nenv\\n" | ./hsh
.fi
.PP
Commands can also be read from a file:
.PP
.nf
$ ./hsh < commands.txt
.fi
.PP
After reaching end-of-file,
.B hsh
returns the status of the last command.
A command that cannot be found sets the status to 127, but the shell
continues reading any remaining input lines.
.SH COMMAND RESOLUTION
After parsing the input,
.B hsh
checks whether the command is a built-in.
External commands are resolved in one of two ways.
.SS Direct paths
If
.B argv[0]
contains a slash, the entire command name is treated as a path.
The shell checks that exact path instead of searching
.BR PATH .
.PP
Examples include:
.PP
.nf
/bin/ls
\&./program
directory/program
.fi
.PP
Paths beginning with a slash are absolute.
Paths such as
.B ./program
and
.B directory/program
are relative to the current working directory.
.SS PATH search
If
.B argv[0]
does not contain a slash, the shell finds
.B PATH
in the current environment.
It splits the value into directories, combines each directory with the
command name, and checks the resulting paths.
When a matching path is found, its full path is passed to
.BR execve (2).
.PP
If no command is found,
.B hsh
prints an error containing its invocation name, the input line number,
and the command name.
It sets the command status to 127 and does not call
.BR fork (2).
.SH BUILT-IN COMMANDS
.TP
.B exit
Free the current command data and terminate the shell using the status
of the previous command.
This version does not accept a numeric exit-status argument.
.TP
.B env
Print each entry in the current environment.
This command runs directly in the shell without creating a child process.
.SH SIGNAL HANDLING
In interactive mode,
.B hsh
handles SIGINT, normally produced by pressing Ctrl+C.
Instead of terminating, the shell prints a newline and displays a fresh
prompt.
.PP
External programs retain their normal signal behaviour.
For example, pressing Ctrl+C while
.B /bin/sleep 20
is running interrupts that command and returns control to the shell.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B hsh
returns the status of the last command when it reaches end-of-file or
when the
.B exit
built-in is used.
.TP
.B 0
The last command completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
An allocation, process-management, signal-setup, or execution error occurred.
.TP
.B 127
The command could not be found.
.TP
.B 128+n
The child process was terminated by signal
.IR n .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PATH
A colon-separated list of directories searched when a command name does
not contain a slash.
.TP
.B environ
The inherited process environment passed to external commands and printed
by the
.B env
built-in.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start an interactive session:
.PP
.nf
$ ./hsh
shell$ ls -l
shell$ /bin/pwd
shell$ env
shell$ exit
.fi
.PP
Run commands non-interactively:
.PP
.nf
$ printf "pwd\\nls -l\\n" | ./hsh
.fi
.SH LIMITATIONS
This version does not interpret quoting, escaping, wildcard expansion,
variables, pipelines, redirection operators, command separators, or job
control.
Only the
.B exit
and
.B env
built-in commands are implemented.
